1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,751
You've ridden

2
00:00:00,751 --> 00:00:01,386
an elevator

3
00:00:01,386 --> 00:00:02,367
a thousand times.

4
00:00:02,447 --> 00:00:03,343
But what's actually

5
00:00:03,343 --> 00:00:04,051
holding you UP?

6
00:00:04,211 --> 00:00:05,022
Most elevators

7
00:00:05,022 --> 00:00:06,065
don't PUSH you up.

8
00:00:06,145 --> 00:00:06,940
They PULL you.

9
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:08,021
Steel ropes run

10
00:00:08,021 --> 00:00:08,819
from the car,

11
00:00:08,819 --> 00:00:10,231
up over a grooved wheel

12
00:00:10,231 --> 00:00:11,213
called a sheave,

13
00:00:11,213 --> 00:00:12,012
and down to a

14
00:00:12,012 --> 00:00:12,871
counterweight.

15
00:00:13,031 --> 00:00:14,284
That counterweight

16
00:00:14,284 --> 00:00:15,258
weighs as much

17
00:00:15,258 --> 00:00:16,023
as the car,

18
00:00:16,023 --> 00:00:16,998
plus about 40%

19
00:00:16,998 --> 00:00:18,042
of a full load.

20
00:00:18,202 --> 00:00:18,810
So the motor

21
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:19,418
ISN'T really

22
00:00:19,418 --> 00:00:20,026
lifting you.

23
00:00:20,106 --> 00:00:20,917
It's just tipping

24
00:00:20,917 --> 00:00:21,489
the balance.

25
00:00:21,649 --> 00:00:22,445
Friction between

26
00:00:22,445 --> 00:00:23,639
the rope and the grooves

27
00:00:23,639 --> 00:00:24,336
does the rest.

28
00:00:24,416 --> 00:00:25,315
That's TRACTION.

29
00:00:25,475 --> 00:00:26,077
But what if

30
00:00:26,077 --> 00:00:26,789
a rope SNAPS?

31
00:00:26,869 --> 00:00:27,589
You're hanging

32
00:00:27,589 --> 00:00:28,670
from several of them,

33
00:00:28,670 --> 00:00:29,493
built many times

34
00:00:29,493 --> 00:00:30,162
stronger than

35
00:00:30,162 --> 00:00:30,985
they need to be.

36
00:00:31,145 --> 00:00:31,976
And if the car

37
00:00:31,976 --> 00:00:33,162
ever falls too fast,

38
00:00:33,162 --> 00:00:34,171
a governor trips,

39
00:00:34,171 --> 00:00:35,120
and brakes CLAMP

40
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:36,010
onto the rails.

41
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:36,915
Elisha Otis

42
00:00:36,915 --> 00:00:38,133
proved it in 1854,

43
00:00:38,133 --> 00:00:39,623
by having the rope cut

44
00:00:39,623 --> 00:00:40,570
while he stood

45
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:41,653
on the platform.

46
00:00:41,813 --> 00:00:42,430
Oh, and that

47
00:00:42,430 --> 00:00:43,355
CLOSE DOOR button?

48
00:00:43,435 --> 00:00:44,380
On many elevators,

49
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:45,324
it's switched OFF.

50
00:00:45,404 --> 00:00:46,279
It's really there

51
00:00:46,279 --> 00:00:47,153
for firefighters.

52
00:00:47,313 --> 00:00:48,160
Follow for more

53
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:48,950
things you use

54
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:49,515
every day.

